{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Behold now, I have two daughters</b> — From the praise of this man we have come to his disgrace, for he exerted himself greatly on behalf of his guests to save them because they had come under the shelter of his roof, but to appease the men of the city by handing over his daughters — this is nothing but evil of heart, for the matter of lust concerning women was not far removed in his eyes, and he did not consider that he was doing great violence to his daughters. Therefore our Sages said (Tanhuma Vayera 12): By normal practice in the world a man sacrifices himself for his daughters and his wife, and is either killed or kills, but this one hands over his daughters to be violated. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: For yourself you are safeguarding them.\n\nNow Lot feared greatly on their account, for he thought they were men. But when they struck the men of the city with blindness and said to him, 'For we are about to destroy this place, and HaShem has sent us to destroy it,' then he recognized them and believed to do all that they commanded.\n\nKnow and understand that the incident of the concubine in Gibeah (Judges 19), although it resembles this incident, is not equal to it in wickedness. For those wicked men had no intention of eliminating the sojourner from their midst, but were steeped in lust and desired even the bed of the male guest; and when the concubine was brought out to them, they were appeased by her. And the old man who said to them, 'Here is my daughter, a virgin, and his concubine; let me bring them out now, and do to them what is good in your eyes' (Judges 19:24) — he knew that they would not desire his daughter and would do no harm to her; therefore they refused to listen to him, and when he brought out his concubine alone, they left him alone. And the host and the guest alike were all desirous of saving the man by means of his concubine, for a concubine was not a married woman, and she had already committed adultery against him.\n\nFurthermore, in that incident not all the people of the city were involved, as in Sodom, concerning whom it is written, 'from young to old, all the people from every quarter.' But concerning Gibeah it is written, 'the men of the city, worthless men' (Judges 19:22) — a portion of them who were leaders and powerful men in the city, as the man said (Judges 20:5), 'the lords of Gibeah rose up against me'; therefore the others did not protest with their hand.\n\nNow all the people from all the tribes of Israel resolved to make a great fence in this matter to put them to death, as it says (Judges 20:13), 'Now deliver the worthless men, the sons of Belial, who are in Gibeah, that we may put them to death.' Yet it is clear that they were not liable to the death penalty according to Torah law, for they did nothing besides abusing the concubine who was a zonah, and they did not intend her death, nor did she die by their hand. Rather, they sent her away from them at the rising of dawn; she went from them to her master's house and only afterward died — perhaps she was weakened from the abundance of bodily discharge and collapsed at the doorway until morning and died there. But because they were desirous and declared their intent to commit outrage like the men of Sodom, the tribes saw fit to make a fence for the Torah, that such a thing should neither be done nor said in Israel, as they said, 'We will burn out the evil from Israel' (ibid.).\n\nThis ruling is among what our Sages said (Sanhedrin 46a): a court may flog and punish not according to Torah law, not to transgress the words of the Torah but to make a fence for the Torah. And the tribe of Benjamin did not agree in this matter, for they held that there was no liability of death for the abuse of the concubine. Perhaps the Benjaminites were additionally indignant that the others had not sent word to them at the outset, and that the agreement was reached without their knowledge.\n\nIn my opinion, this was the punishment of Israel — to be smitten at the beginning — because the war was not waged justly, and the fence itself was incumbent upon the tribe of Benjamin to make and not upon them, for there is a commandment upon a tribe to judge its own tribe (Sifrei 144).\n\nNow both factions deserved punishment: Benjamin, for countenancing wickedness, neither restraining the evildoers nor rebuking them at all; and Israel, for waging war not according to law, and also for not inquiring of HaShem's mouth in this matter, but rather saying, 'Who shall go up for us first to battle against the children of Benjamin?' (Judges 20:18), for they themselves had agreed to war in any case. Nor did they inquire regarding the matter of victory — 'Will You deliver them into my hand?' — for they trusted in the arm of flesh, since they were extremely numerous, about ten times their number and more. They asked only, 'Who shall go up for us first?' — and this was like a casting of lots among them. Perhaps each tribe would say, 'I will not go up first,' or would say, 'I am first,' and the Holy One, blessed be He, answered according to their question: 'Judah first' — meaning that Judah is at the head forever, 'for HaShem chose Judah to be prince' (1 Chronicles 28:4). Therefore He did not say 'Judah shall go up,' as in other places (cf. Judges 1:2); for He did not authorize them, yet neither did He prevent them, nor did He say to them, 'Do not go up and do not fight' — because of Benjamin's punishment.\n\nThus HaShem went with both of them בקרי (as an adversary) and left them to chance. The Benjaminites were mighty men and their cities were fortified, and they cut down Israel, who trusted in the arm of flesh, and added punishment upon their punishment. For it would have sufficed them to rout Israel from Gibeah, but they struck them to destroy — with eternal hatred — and felled from them a great and mighty people, twenty-two thousand.\n\nNow when Israel was struck a great blow, their error became known to them — that they had waged war against their brethren without authorization from on high and not according to Torah law. Therefore on the second day they asked, 'Shall I again draw near to battle against Benjamin my brother?' (Judges 20:23), and now they mentioned brotherhood, to ask whether it forbade them from war. And HaShem authorized them now on the second day, and said, 'Go up against him' — for now it was permitted to them to avenge their brothers' blood. Yet they did not inquire regarding victory, for they still trusted in their great numbers to prevail in any case, and HaShem only indicated to them that the war was permitted. But because their first punishment was not yet atoned for, eighteen thousand of them fell also on the second day.\n\nOn the third day they decreed a fast and they fasted and wept before HaShem, and they offered burnt offerings to atone for the musings of the heart that had trusted in their arm, and they offered peace offerings — and these were thanksgiving offerings — for they saw themselves as though all of them had escaped from the sword of Benjamin. This is the rule for all who escape danger: to offer a thanksgiving offering, as it says (Psalms 107:22), 'Let them sacrifice sacrifices of thanksgiving and declare His deeds with song,' and it is written (Psalms 27:6), 'And now my head shall be lifted up above my enemies around me, and I will offer in His tent sacrifices of joy; I will sing and make music to HaShem.'\n\nNow over the two days the dead of Israel numbered forty thousand, and of Benjamin there fell in the end twenty-five thousand men of valor (Judges 20:35), and in the city from the dead to all who were found (Judges 20:48) there were many — perhaps fifteen thousand among men, women, and children — so the punishment of both factions was equal.\n\nHow weighty are the words of our Sages (Sanhedrin 103b) that the wrath was because of the idol of Micah: the Holy One, blessed be He, said, 'In My honor you did not protest; in honor of flesh and blood you protested' — to say: in My honor you did not protest, regarding those liable to death who laid hands on the root; in honor of flesh and blood you protested beyond the line of justice. Therefore He confounded the counsel of both factions and hardened their hearts, and they did not remember the covenant of brotherhood. And after the deed they regretted it, as it says (Judges 21:2–3), 'The people came to Bethel and sat there until evening before HaElohim, and lifted up their voices and wept with a great weeping, and said: Why, HaShem Elohim of Israel, has this happened in Israel, that there should be lacking from Israel today one tribe?' — for they recognized their error and their punishment.\n\nNow by way of the plain sense we have explained the matter of their essence, and it is not fully clear, and we have recalled its causes:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The verse references to Judges in the source text are given by chapter only (e.g., שופטים יט) or by chapter and verse; I expanded all to full chapter:verse form based on the cited content. The phrase בקרי (Leviticus 26:21, 'in hostility' / 'as an adversary') is left in Hebrew as Ramban uses it technically."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:32:39.658232968Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}